 @mopey39  dude, everyone has that insight. just go look at the GPU database on toms... it's TSMC 5nm, it's 272mm^2, and it's a 192 bit bus. the 4060ti is on the same node with a 128 bit bus and 188mm^2. so the silicon cost is already way lower for a 4060ti, additionally the nvidia GPU uses less power, so you get away with a smaller VRM and a smaller cooling solution. 

it's obvious that intel is barely making money on these. it was the same thing with alchemist. intel has to use way more silicon to achieve the same performance. which is expected, but it means GPUs won't be profitable for them for a good while to come, especially as their R&D costs will be proportionally bigger since they have way less of a base to build on. and on top of that, intel pissed off TSMC, and as a result TSMC canceled a discount on their fab allocations as a result. so potentially, intel needs 44% more silicon to get the same or slightly worse performance, and is also paying more per square mm of the same node than AMD and nVidia are...

all that info is readily available, it's not rocket science.

